#achievement(title: [Chromatin Accessibility Reproduces the Broad Neuronal Cell-Type Enrichment (Same-Analyst, Unpublished)])[
*Certainty: 0.64 (cross-modality confirmation; the ME/CFS-specific application is unpublished).* A second molecular modality---chromatin accessibility---was applied to the same DecodeME ME/CFS GWAS, using the same stratified LD score regression (S-LDSC) pipeline by the same investigator, against the Li et al.\ (2023) single-nucleus ATAC-seq atlas of the human brain (1.1 million cells across 42 brain regions, organised into 107 cell types) @Li2023ATACbrain. This atlas measures chromatin openness rather than gene expression. The analysis returned the same *coarse* pattern previously seen with RNA atlases: enrichment in neurons rather than immune cells. The specific top cell type, however, differs by reference: where the RNA atlases (Siletti/Human Brain Atlas and Dropviz) highlighted striatal medium spiny neurons, the chromatin atlas's strongest signal falls on intratelencephalic (IT)-projecting cortical neurons (subtypes IT-L4 and IT-V1C), with a medium spiny neuron (MSN) peak present but secondary. Only the broad neuronal-versus-immune distinction---not a single specific subtype---is reproduced across modalities, and because the same analyst and the same pipeline were used, agreement does not rule out a shared pipeline- or annotation-level explanation.

The method is not generically biased toward neurons: prior snATAC+GWAS studies recover disease-specific cell types---glutamatergic neurons for schizophrenia @Hauberg2020openChromatin and microglia (with an oligodendrocyte-lineage component) for Alzheimer's and Parkinson's disease @Corces2020snATACcausalVariants ---so a neuronal result for ME/CFS is at least consistent with the pipeline behaving as intended. The analyst reported reproducing Li et al.'s own published schizophrenia and tiredness coefficients and p-values at r ≈ 0.99; this calibrates their S-LDSC setup against the atlas paper's known outputs, but it says nothing about the validity of the ME/CFS result itself.

Three caveats bound this finding. First, the ME/CFS-specific enrichment is a single-investigator, unpublished analysis documented on a blog and forum thread, not a peer-reviewed result---the atlas resource is peer-reviewed, but every ME/CFS interpretation traces to one analysis and has not been independently replicated. Second, the atlas derives from only 3 brain donors, which limits generality of the reference cell types. Third, the MSN peak is not ME/CFS-specific: it is shared with substance-use and other brain traits, and it is *absent* for self-reported tiredness (the Deary 2018 UK Biobank tiredness GWAS @Deary2018tiredness, as re-analysed in this unpublished Li-atlas enrichment). Fine-grained cell-type inference also remains method-dependent @Jagadeesh2022scDRS @Yu2021limitedAssociation (Limitation @lim:cell-type-enrichment-caveats). None of these caveats changes the direction of the evidence: across two molecular modalities and multiple atlases, ME/CFS genetic risk enriches in neurons, not immune cells.

*Consequence:* Two kinds of molecular data---RNA expression and chromatin accessibility---point to the same broad conclusion: the ME/CFS genetic signal is carried by brain neurons, not immune cells. This strengthens the existing brain-first genetic model @hyp:brain-first-genetic rather than changing it, and does not yet alter any diagnosis or treatment. It mainly tells researchers that the broad neuronal signal is unlikely to be a one-method artefact, so neuronal-circuit mechanisms deserve priority as readouts of genetic risk---while the specific neuronal subtype remains unresolved.

Study: (snATAC atlas n=1.1M cells / 3 donors, 42 regions, 107 cell types; DecodeME ME/CFS S-LDSC, single-investigator, unpublished; broad neuronal-versus-immune pattern convergent with RNA atlases @Siletti2023transcriptomicBrain).
] <ach:chromatin-accessibility-enrichment>
